1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,384
One ship. Four official costs. None of them agree.

2
00:00:03,624 --> 00:00:12,913
The aircraft carrier John F. Kennedy, CVN-79, has a 2019 budget estimate of 11.3 billion dollars,

3
00:00:12,913 --> 00:00:24,308
a cost cap in law of 11.4 billion, a cost limitation baseline of 12.9 billion, and a 2027 budget total of 13.4 billion.

4
00:00:24,548 --> 00:00:26,762
Each figure is correct for its own purpose.

5
00:00:27,002 --> 00:00:33,172
Today, no office can trace what a weapon system costs from the budget request, through the contracts,

6
00:00:33,172 --> 00:00:40,075
to the asset, in a form that a program office, a cost estimator, a budget analyst and an auditor will all accept.

7
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:43,304
Our answer is one solution in four layers.

8
00:00:43,544 --> 00:00:49,875
At the base, MCP A Creator builds a tested, governed MCP server from a question and a table.

9
00:00:50,115 --> 00:00:56,812
Above it, domain MCP servers give agents cited access to documents, records and registers.

10
00:00:57,052 --> 00:01:01,843
The agent orchestration environment is where people and agents work the questions.

11
00:01:02,083 --> 00:01:08,480
At the top, a financial-control layer decides what is true and who may act. No model is in that layer.

12
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:14,459
The differentiator is AI assisted development, joined to deterministic financial control.

13
00:01:15,167 --> 00:01:22,044
All of it runs on Databricks today, as seven apps deployed from the public GitHub repository with one command.

14
00:01:22,284 --> 00:01:31,502
Five of them are MCP servers, with 56 tools: MCP A Creator, the docs server, the analyst server, the ops server, and the One Hub.

15
00:01:31,742 --> 00:01:36,980
The other two apps are the front center, and a probe that calls every server every fifteen minutes.

16
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:46,001
A sixth server, built by the Creator on an unrelated sales table, stays in the repository as proof that the Creator is not tied to ships.

17
00:01:46,733 --> 00:01:52,811
This is the front center. The start page states the solution: four layers and seven capabilities.

18
00:01:53,051 --> 00:02:02,468
Each of the three GSA tracks is served by a different layer: dataset access, service read integration, and service write integration in a sandbox.

19
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:07,293
The financial-control layer shows how a figure earns its place in a brief.

20
00:02:07,533 --> 00:02:13,249
Code analyzes the records in exact decimals. Code reconciles each figure to its source.

21
00:02:13,489 --> 00:02:20,870
Code cites the document, page, table and cell. A gate routes the figure. Then a person in the owning role decides.

22
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:27,695
A model may plan, explain and draft. A model can never add a figure, change an amount, or approve anything.

23
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:33,638
Below are the seven capabilities, with what each one solves, for whom, and where it stands today.

24
00:02:33,878 --> 00:02:41,303
MCP A Creator. The docs server, which reads 34 PDFs and 7,804 pages down to the page and

25
00:02:41,303 --> 00:02:47,462
the table. The analyst server, which works a record that is missing data.

26
00:02:47,702 --> 00:02:54,778
The ops server, which the Creator built. The One Hub server, which holds the gate. The probe. And the front center itself.

27
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:59,727
The same page shows how it was tested, and what each result does not show.

28
00:02:59,967 --> 00:03:07,305
An outside evaluator's questions scored 83 percent. A blind reviewer would rely on nine of ten answers.

29
00:03:07,545 --> 00:03:20,487
7,743 of 7,746 budget tie-outs pass, and the probe got 1,234 of 1,234 known answers right.

30
00:03:20,727 --> 00:03:24,751
Security is listed by risk, with the control in code for each one.

31
00:03:25,467 --> 00:03:33,414
The Impact page makes the case for scale. One carrier, read to the cent. The same agents and servers for every weapon system.

32
00:03:33,654 --> 00:03:36,278
The portfolio is large and slow moving:

33
00:03:36,278 --> 00:03:44,150
more than 2.4 trillion dollars across the department's costliest weapon programs, and its cost cannot yet be audited.

34
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:50,900
The working pattern is simple. You ask. Agents plan. MCP servers fetch. The gate checks. You decide.

35
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:57,389
Five users bring five different questions: a senior leader, a program office cost analyst,

36
00:03:57,389 --> 00:04:02,084
a budget analyst, an accountant or auditor, and any analyst with a table.

37
00:04:02,324 --> 00:04:05,490
Each question is traced through the stack, tool by tool.

38
00:04:05,730 --> 00:04:14,429
With MCP A Creator, a new program gets its own governed server without a developer. Each new program supplies data, not software.

39
00:04:15,133 --> 00:04:22,902
The same servers already read the whole Ford class. Four hulls come from budget books that are already loaded, each with its cost cap.

40
00:04:23,142 --> 00:04:28,140
The page records what CVN-78 and CVN-79 teach the ships that follow,

41
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:34,241
and the eight steps that take any program from the public record to governed tools.

42
00:04:34,967 --> 00:04:41,085
The plan runs program by program, in phases: the Ford class, Navy shipbuilding,

43
00:04:41,085 --> 00:04:47,281
the F-35, and then the 59 programs that have a 2027 selected acquisition report.

44
00:04:47,521 --> 00:04:51,723
The page ends by saying what it does not show, and by listing its sources.

45
00:04:52,433 --> 00:04:58,175
The Brief page answers one question: what can we say about CVN-79's cost today?

46
00:04:58,415 --> 00:05:03,289
You start from your own question. The page then shows what you can say, with its basis.

47
00:05:03,529 --> 00:05:10,812
A total ship estimate of 13.41 billion dollars. 17.7 percent above the statutory cap.

48
00:05:10,812 --> 00:05:18,609
75.8 percent traced to a named contract. And a delivery 54 months later than first planned.

49
00:05:18,849 --> 00:05:24,066
It also lists what to hold back, and why, and which open questions still block the number.

50
00:05:24,767 --> 00:05:29,915
The ship page gives the context: the second ship of the Gerald R. Ford class,

51
00:05:29,915 --> 00:05:35,330
built at HII Newport News Shipbuilding, with every milestone cited to its source.

52
00:05:36,033 --> 00:05:40,338
Under Cost, the Position page answers which figure to quote, and for what.

53
00:05:40,578 --> 00:05:48,901
The four published figures are four different kinds of number: a dated estimate, a limit in law, an adjusted limit, and a budget request.

54
00:05:49,141 --> 00:05:52,780
The bridge from the cap to the budget closes exactly,

55
00:05:52,780 --> 00:06:01,429
step by step. The budget total sits 2.01 billion dollars above the statutory cap, and each step names the mechanism behind it.

56
00:06:01,669 --> 00:06:06,954
What cannot yet be explained is stated as an open question, with the office that can close it.

57
00:06:07,667 --> 00:06:13,273
The reconciliation ladder shows how far down the records go, from appropriation to disbursement.

58
00:06:13,513 --> 00:06:20,085
Cost composition shows what a named contract or vendor line accounts for. 75.8 percent

59
00:06:20,085 --> 00:06:26,657
of the hull is tied to a named record. The remaining 24.2 percent is a visibility gap,

60
00:06:26,657 --> 00:06:27,956
not a budget gap.

61
00:06:28,196 --> 00:06:36,080
The estimate history shows how the estimate moved across twelve budget submissions, and the statutory cap history sits beside it.

62
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:46,398
Budget execution is computed live from 1,875 prime contract transactions and 2,990 sub-awards.

63
00:06:46,638 --> 00:06:54,429
Now a live specialist run. We ask the Account Analyst to explain why one File C period does not tie to the transactions.

64
00:06:54,669 --> 00:07:03,768
Every step is labeled as code or as model. Code routes the task. Code packs the records in exact decimals. One model explains them.

65
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:09,424
Then code checks the answer again. The canvas re-check passes ten of ten conditions.

66
00:07:09,664 --> 00:07:15,224
A second model family, the Records Checker, confirms that every stated fact matches a record.

67
00:07:15,464 --> 00:07:20,392
Two proposals are ready for a person. Nothing is saved until a person applies it.

68
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:30,919
In review, the first proposal belongs to the Navy's Office of Financial Operations. We are signed in as program office PMS 379,

69
00:07:30,919 --> 00:07:33,702
so the system refuses, and says why.

70
00:07:33,942 --> 00:07:41,152
The role check runs in Unity Catalog, under the user's own sign-in. No role travels in a page or a cookie.

71
00:07:41,392 --> 00:07:45,107
The second proposal belongs to our own office, so we can apply it.

72
00:07:45,833 --> 00:07:51,052
Back on the page, the obligation curve shows the handoff between the two prime contracts,

73
00:07:51,052 --> 00:07:55,039
and the largest contract actions are listed with their descriptions.

74
00:07:55,767 --> 00:08:01,650
Under Audit, the gap register answers which open questions block the number, and who can close them.

75
00:08:01,890 --> 00:08:08,687
65 questions are registered. 59 are open, and 14 can be worked now with no new access.

76
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:16,200
A second specialist, the Gap Investigator, works one gap: when is construction in progress relieved for this hull?

77
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:24,415
It reads the collection requirements, names the office that holds the answer, and marks what it could not confirm as blank, instead of guessing.

78
00:08:24,655 --> 00:08:28,771
The same checks pass, and one request is ready for a person to review.

79
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:35,960
The variance register keeps every place where public sources disagree. Conflicting values are retained with

80
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:39,100
their provenance, and never resolved by picking one.

81
00:08:39,340 --> 00:08:50,528
One example: a December 2025 baseline of 13.529 billion dollars is printed, but not yet confirmed. The gate holds it for human review.

82
00:08:51,233 --> 00:08:57,419
Asset valuation looks at the hull as the Navy's books see it: construction in progress, not yet a vessel.

83
00:08:57,659 --> 00:09:05,536
The page gives three structural reasons why capitalized value will never equal program cost: scope, timing and boundary.

84
00:09:06,267 --> 00:09:15,452
The industrial base page shows who builds what. The shipyard is the hull builder and the systems integrator, but not the maker of most major systems.

85
00:09:15,692 --> 00:09:27,186
Tier two comes from 2,990 reported sub-awards: 426.8 million dollars, 261 subcontractors, in 35 states.

86
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:37,975
Ask the evidence is an MCP host, not a chatbot. The agent lists the tools of three MCP servers, and sends each call to the server that owns it.

87
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:45,605
We ask two questions in plain language: why are there three different cost caps, and which File C periods fail to tie?

88
00:09:45,845 --> 00:09:50,016
Each answer comes back as a lead, with its citations, its tool calls,

89
00:09:50,016 --> 00:09:55,456
and the servers that answered. Where the tools cannot support a claim, the answer says so.

90
00:09:55,696 --> 00:09:59,777
An answer here is a starting point to verify, never a reported figure.

91
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:04,169
The work queue shows what needs a decision, what is blocked, and why.

92
00:10:04,409 --> 00:10:13,151
16 decisions are waiting. Where no one holds the required role, the queue says so, and the item waits. It is never applied by default.

93
00:10:13,867 --> 00:10:22,904
Brief readiness checks the claim ledger: 13 of 32 CVN-79 figures tie to source records today. The rest stay leads.

94
00:10:23,633 --> 00:10:29,640
The Obligation Reconciler tests a contract's reported cost against its recorded obligations.

95
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:37,492
We give it a figure to test: five million dollars. Code sums 927 contract actions in exact decimals,

96
00:10:37,492 --> 00:10:42,592
finds 4.58 billion dollars, and reports the difference to the cent.

97
00:10:42,832 --> 00:10:45,440
Each File C period is then compared with

98
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:53,915
the transactions. Periods that tie are confirmed. Periods that do not are marked as a residual, with a button to explain each one.

99
00:10:54,155 --> 00:10:57,974
Again, two proposals wait for a person, and the run is recorded.

100
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:07,959
The run log keeps every run, and every action by every person. The detail table holds the records behind the figures, with filters and export.

101
00:11:08,199 --> 00:11:12,703
The sources page says what each document family carries, and what it does not.

102
00:11:13,433 --> 00:11:20,493
The method page explains how this is built, and why it can be challenged. It keeps what the method got wrong, on purpose.

103
00:11:20,733 --> 00:11:27,740
It also shows where AI sits. No AI output writes directly to a trusted table, and the gate decides in code.

104
00:11:28,467 --> 00:11:37,238
The last page turns to the people who hold the answers. There are 59 open questions, and two sentences from the right person can close one.

105
00:11:37,478 --> 00:11:44,506
You pick a question in your area, and read the brief the system prepared. You say what you know, how you know it, and how sure you are.

106
00:11:44,746 --> 00:11:50,555
Your answer is recorded as a lead, with your name. It is never recorded as a certified figure.

107
00:11:51,267 --> 00:11:59,546
How do we know it works? 1,770 tests pass from a clean clone, and 828 of them are hostile inputs.

108
00:11:59,786 --> 00:12:04,805
421 hostile calls against the 30 read tools wrote nothing.

109
00:12:05,045 --> 00:12:10,898
On sealed questions, tools take two model families from guessing to answering:

110
00:12:10,898 --> 00:12:17,651
Claude from 14 percent correct to 88 percent, and GPT-5 mini from 1 percent to 79 percent.

111
00:12:17,891 --> 00:12:25,326
On an outside evaluator's questions, Claude scored 83 percent. Neither outside person is on the development team.

112
00:12:26,033 --> 00:12:33,448
Controls are in code, not in prompts. Every app sits behind workspace sign-in, and runs as its own service principal.

113
00:12:33,688 --> 00:12:39,567
Inputs are validated, SQL parameters are bound, and retrieved text is treated as data.

114
00:12:39,807 --> 00:12:48,653
An outside scan on November 23rd found that an unexpected error could carry internal text to a client. Every server now withholds it,

115
00:12:48,653 --> 00:12:50,050
and a test checks it.

116
00:12:50,767 --> 00:12:58,279
The next program supplies data, not software. The Creator, the readers, the gate and the front center are reused as they are.

117
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:03,668
AI builds and explains. Code proves the number. The owning role decides.

118
00:13:03,908 --> 00:13:07,710
One ship, reconciled to the cent, and ready for the next program.

